---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import { routePath } from '../../i18n/routes';

const locale = 'en' as const;
const p = (key: Parameters<typeof routePath>[1]) => routePath(locale, key);

const features = [
  { label: 'INTENT', title: 'Search intent', text: 'Each page answers one search. We work out, page by page, which search each of your pages serves.' },
  { label: 'TITLE', title: 'Title and description', text: 'The first two lines people see in search. We put the service and district near the start and say honestly what the page contains.' },
  { label: 'LINKS', title: 'Internal links', text: 'We link service pages to each other and to district pages. A page that gets no links from anywhere looks unimportant to Google.' },
  { label: 'OVERLAP', title: 'Page overlap', text: 'If two of your pages target the same search, both fall behind. We merge the pages or separate their targets.' },
];

const toc = [
  { id: 'nedir', label: 'What does on-page SEO mean?' },
  { id: 'niyet', label: 'How many searches does a page answer?' },
  { id: 'title', label: 'Title and description' },
  { id: 'yapi', label: 'What belongs on the page?' },
  { id: 'link', label: 'Internal links' },
  { id: 'cakisma', label: 'Pages competing for the same search' },
  { id: 'yerel', label: 'How a district name goes on the page' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'How many times should the keyword appear on the page?',
    a: 'Don’t count. Copy that does the service justice naturally includes the phrases around that service. Readers and Google both notice copy that chases keyword density.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Google rewrites my title. Is it worth writing one?',
    a: 'It is. Google changes the title mostly when it’s missing, too long or doesn’t fit the page. We write the title so it says the same thing as the H1 and the content.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does a longer page rank better?',
    a: 'No. A page that answers the question briefly and completely can get ahead of one that stretches the same thing out. What we look at is whether every question the reader asks has an answer on the page.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should we fix existing pages or write new ones?',
    a: 'We fix the existing ones first. Pages that get impressions but no clicks, or that are stuck on page two, are the ones that move fastest. New pages come after these gaps are closed.',
  },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  locale={locale}
  route="services/on-page-seo"
  title="On-Page SEO Service | Wiserrise"
  description="We make clear which search each page answers: titles, headings, internal links and overlapping pages. On-page SEO for local business websites."
  eyebrow="SERVICE · ON-PAGE SEO"
  h1="Fit every page to the right search with on-page SEO."
  crumb="On-page SEO"
  lede="On-page SEO is the work of telling Google clearly which search each page on your site answers. We fix the page itself, from the title and headings to the copy and internal links. For a local business, the goal is that someone searching “Brisbane physiotherapy” lands on the page that describes exactly that service in Brisbane."
  serviceName="On-Page SEO"
  breadcrumb={[
    { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
    { label: 'Services', href: p('services') },
    { label: 'SEO', href: p('services/seo') },
  ]}
  features={features}
  toc={toc}
  faq={faq}
  related={[
    { label: 'SEO', href: p('services/seo'), text: 'Groundwork, page structure, content and links: the main service this one belongs to.' },
    { label: 'Technical SEO', href: p('services/technical-seo'), text: 'We fix crawling and indexing errors so your site opens properly to Google.' },
    { label: 'Content Production', href: p('services/content'), text: 'We write the missing service and district pages and connect them to the internal link structure.' },
    { label: 'Local SEO', href: p('services/local-seo'), text: 'We fix your profile and your site together and work on getting you to stand out on the map.' },
  ]}
  ctaLabel="Get a free analysis"
  ctaHref="/#contact"
>
  <h2 id="nedir">What does on-page SEO mean?</h2>

  <p>
    SEO work done on the page itself. Title, description, headings, copy and internal links are its material. There’s one
    goal: when Google looks at the page, it should understand without hesitation which search the page answers.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href={p('services/technical-seo')}>Technical SEO</a> makes sure the page reaches Google; on-page SEO decides what it
    says once it gets there. Each is half done without the other, so we run both in the same plan.
  </p>

  <h2 id="niyet">How many searches does a page answer?</h2>

  <p>
    One. Say you’re a lawyer in Columbus who handles divorce cases. Someone typing “how long does a divorce case take” wants
    information; someone typing “divorce lawyer Columbus” is looking for a lawyer.
  </p>

  <p>
    A site that tries to serve both on one page falls behind on both. So don’t squeeze two services or two intents into one
    page. The service page describes the service, a separate guide article is written for the person looking for
    information, and the two link to each other.
  </p>

  <p>
    We learned about intent with our own money, on Google Ads. Without noticing, we paid for searches from people who only
    wanted information and had no intention of buying a service; a real share of the budget was gone before we pulled back from the edge.
    Since then we ask one question of every page first: what does the person making this search want?
  </p>

  <h2 id="title">How do you write a title and description?</h2>

  <p>
    The title is the strongest on-page signal telling Google what the page is about. It’s also the blue line people click in
    search results. What we look at when writing it:
  </p>

  <ul class="checklist">
    <li>The search phrase sits near the start, without being forced</li>
    <li>For a local service, the district or city name appears</li>
    <li>The brand name comes at the end and stays short</li>
    <li>It stays short enough not to get cut off on the results page</li>
    <li>Every page on the site has its own title; no two pages share one</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The meta description is the short text below the title on the results page. Searchers decide whether to click based on
    it, and we write it as an honest summary of what’s really on the page. A description that promises something the page
    doesn’t deliver sends the visitor back to the results page within seconds.
  </p>

  <h2 id="yapi">What should be on the page?</h2>

  <p>
    A single H1, and below it the questions the reader will ask. The H1 says on its own what the page is about; the H2s
    split the topic into parts along the reader’s questions.
  </p>

  <p>The headings we look for on a local service page:</p>

  <ul class="checklist">
    <li>What the service is and who it suits</li>
    <li>How it works and how long it takes</li>
    <li>Which districts you serve, or how to reach you</li>
    <li>The questions customers ask most on the phone</li>
    <li>Phone, address and a way to book, reachable from the first screen</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    We don’t pick these headings by guessing. We look at the searches the page appears in on Search Console and at the
    questions you hear on the phone. Any question that comes up in either and has no answer on the page counts as missing.
  </p>

  <h2 id="link">Why do internal links matter?</h2>

  <p>
    Internal links draw Google a map of the site and point out the important pages. The most common gap we see on local
    business sites is service pages that don’t link to each other at all. Every page stands alone, like an island.
  </p>

  <p>
    The structure we build is simple. The services page links to every service page, and each service page links back to it
    and to the two or three services closest in topic. District pages link to the related service page, and guide articles
    link to the service they cover.
  </p>

  <p>
    The link text says what the target page is about; we don’t write “click here”. The related services block at the bottom
    of this page is built on the same logic.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cakisma">Are two of your pages competing for the same search?</h2>

  <p>
    Then Google can’t decide which one to show and keeps both behind. This is called keyword cannibalization. We find it a
    lot on sites that have been blogging for years.
  </p>

  <p>
    Say your site has separate pages for both “Brisbane physical therapy” and “Brisbane physiotherapy”. Both describe the
    same service to the same person. If Search Console shows one of them for a search one day and the other for the same
    search the next day, that’s the diagnosis.
  </p>

  <p>
    The fix is one of two things. We merge the weaker page into the stronger one and redirect it with a 301, or we truly
    separate the two pages’ targets.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Before opening a new page, ask:</strong> Do I already have a page that answers this search? If so, don’t open
      a new one; deepen the one you have.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="yerel">How does a district name get onto the page?</h2>

  <p>
    Through its natural places. It appears once in the title, the H1 or the intro, and then the copy continues with
    information that really relates to that district. Sprinkling the district name through the copy doesn’t work, and
    readers notice it too.
  </p>

  <p>
    Your pages should also talk to your Google Business Profile. Every service listed on the profile has a page with the
    same name on the site. Name, address and phone are written exactly as on the profile and tied to the same business with
    schema; we handle this part on the <a href={`${p('services/technical-seo')}#schema`}>technical SEO</a> side.
  </p>

  <p>
    Opening a separate page for each district is a separate decision. We only open those pages if there’s something real to
    say about that area; we explain what we look at on the <a href={`${p('services/content')}#sehir`}>content
    production</a> page. How the profile and the site work together is on the
    <a href={p('services/local-seo')}>local SEO</a> page.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="cta">
    <h2>Which searches do your pages <span class="highlight">overlap</span> on?</h2>
    <p>
      The free analysis is a video we record for your business: where you stand today and what to start with first. It
      lands in your inbox within <span style="white-space: nowrap;">24 hours</span>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ContentLayout>
